  "cover_question": "没有共同手语的孩子，怎样创造出一门语言？",
  "reading_note": "",
  "illustration_prompt": "Create a single publication-quality landscape 3:2 narrative illustration for an adult illustrated English-reading booklet. Finished standalone artwork, not a book mockup, not a collage of multiple unrelated panels. Sophisticated contemporary editorial gouache and watercolor, carefully controlled colored-pencil accents, velvety matte paint, subtle warm paper texture, soft natural daylight. Warm ivory paper dominates; subdued eucalyptus and sage greens structure the composition; restrained buttery lemon-yellow and dusty terracotta accents. Calm, comfortable, refined, credible drawing and natural anatomy. An airy coherent composition with one clear story focal point and at most two supporting details, generous breathing room at all edges and softly feathered painted edges into ivory. Detailed but uncluttered. Historically and scientifically appropriate objects, clothing and setting. Show the particular article's pivotal event or mechanism rather than a generic portrait or generic textbook symbols. Human figures treated with dignity; no grotesque caricature, no sensational violence or visible injuries. For disasters, emphasize the setting, evidence or human response without graphic suffering. For uncertain scientific claims, depict the observations and investigation without presenting debated mechanisms as proven. Absolutely no typography, captions, letters, numbers, logos, watermarks, diagram labels, fake writing or invented charts anywhere. Do not render invisible forces as literal magic. Avoid corporate vector icons, slick 3D rendering and childish cartoon style. High-quality, print-ready visual storytelling, matching a warm ivory-and-sage adult watercolor booklet.\n\nARTICLE-SPECIFIC SCENE: Inside a modest late-1970s to early-1980s Managua city bus, two Nicaraguan deaf schoolchildren around ten and thirteen years old sit across a narrow aisle and communicate intently with natural open-hand gestures and lively attentive faces. Their hands are anatomically clear with plausible fingers and relaxed wrists; no attempt to depict a specific codified sign or finger-spelled letter. One additional child further back watches warmly. Plain period shirts, simple sage-green bus seats, ivory daylight through windows, a soft glimpse of an ordinary Managua street. The pivotal story is children creating shared language through peer communication, not a teacher lecturing and not a generic classroom. Dignified, culturally credible children, no exaggerated deafness symbols, no sound effects, no lettering anywhere.",
